# /etc/apivo/<env>/api.env — 0600 root:root
#
# The Go API's own configuration, read by the Docker daemon as the api
# service's `env_file`. It is never sourced by the reconciler and never
# appears in `docker compose config` output, so the database password does
# not travel through a shell that also writes logs.
#
# Every key here is documented in .env.example at the repository root; this
# file records what each one must be ON A DEPLOYED ENVIRONMENT, which is not
# always the same answer.
#
# HTTP_ADDR and APP_ENV are deliberately absent: they are set in
# docker-compose.yml because they are not an operator's choice. APP_ENV is
# `prod` in QA too — see the header of docker-compose.local-db.yml for why
# that is fidelity rather than an error.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL — the one line that differs most between environments.
#
# The api REFUSES TO START if this permits a cleartext session (sslmode
# `disable`, `allow`, `prefer`, or absent). That check is not a formality: it
# exists because every example in this repository ends in sslmode=disable, so
# the value most likely to be pasted into a deployment is the one that must
# never be there.
#
#   qa       the Postgres container on this host, which serves TLS with a
#            self-signed certificate provision.sh generates:
#
#            postgres://apivo:<APIVO_PG_PASSWORD>@postgres:5432/apivo?sslmode=require
#
#            `require` and not `verify-full`: the server is a container one
#            hop away on a network marked internal, and a self-signed
#            certificate cannot prove an identity. Encrypting without
#            claiming to verify is the honest setting; shipping a CA bundle
#            to make a local socket resemble Supabase would not be.
#
#   staging  the STAGING Supabase EU project — its own project, never a
#   prod     schema or a branch inside production's:
#
#            postgres://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/postgres?sslmode=verify-full
#
#            `verify-full` here, which Supabase documents and which also
#            proves the server is the one it claims to be. Anything less on a
#            connection crossing the public internet is a downgrade nobody
#            asked for.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
DATABASE_URL=postgres://apivo:CHANGE-ME@postgres:5432/apivo?sslmode=require

# `info` on every environment. The api emits JSON at APP_ENV=prod, which is
# what makes `journalctl`/`docker logs` output greppable by a person and
# parseable by an agent.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JWT verification for the editorial endpoints. Not a credential: it names
# where the auth provider publishes its PUBLIC keys.
#
# Left EMPTY, every /api/v1/editorial/ route answers 404, the api logs one
# ERROR line saying so, and the reader endpoints keep serving — an editorial
# misconfiguration must not take the public site down. That is why no
# placeholder hostname is shipped here: a placeholder is a real value to the
# binary, and it selects the fail-fast verifier path and crash-loops a fresh
# deploy.
#
#   https://<project>.supabase.co/auth/v1/.well-known/jwks.json
#
# Each environment points at ITS OWN Supabase project. QA pointing at
# staging's project is the kind of thing that is invisible until an editor
# approves a QA article with a staging identity.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
JWKS_URL=
# Optional, and only alongside a non-empty JWKS_URL — setting it alone fails
# startup by design. Supabase access tokens carry: authenticated
JWT_AUDIENCE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Feed polling. Empty means the binary's documented 15m default; "0" disables
# the loop and is the only disable switch.
#
# On Cloudflare this needed a cron trigger to exist at all: the platform
# stops an idle container, which stopped the poll loop with it, so a paper
# quietly stopped updating on a quiet night. A container on a VPS is not
# stopped for being idle, so the loop simply runs. One of the two things this
# move makes strictly simpler.
#
# QA can poll faster than production without costing anyone anything —
# nothing it fetches is published.
#
# For a demo, 1m is the value that works in front of an audience — the same
# pacing docs/SHOWCASE.md sets on the local stack. The procedure for the
# deployed host is "Demo pacing on QA" in docs/RUNBOOK.md; editing THIS
# template changes nothing anywhere, because provision.sh copies it once,
# when the environment is first created.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POLL_INTERVAL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Machine translation. NO DEFAULTS, on purpose: unset means the pipeline
# stays OFF and the api logs one line saying so. The provider and the budget
# are a founder decision and nothing here may make it by accident. Setting
# only some of these keys is a mistake the api names at startup.
#
# The budget is per-environment and the caps are real money. QA runs every
# push to main, so if it translates at all it should carry a small cap of its
# own — never production's, and never a copy of it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
TRANSLATION_BASE_URL=
TRANSLATION_MODEL=
TRANSLATION_API_KEY=
TRANSLATION_INPUT_USD_PER_MTOK=
TRANSLATION_OUTPUT_USD_PER_MTOK=
TRANSLATION_FREE_OF_CHARGE=
TRANSLATION_INTERVAL=
TRANSLATION_ARTICLE_CEILING_MICROUSD=
TRANSLATION_MONTHLY_CAP_MICROUSD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cashback (ADR-0002, ADR-0003). Only the keys an OPERATOR decides are here.
#
# CASHBACK_ENABLED, LEDGER_DRIVER, BLNK_URL and REDIS_URL are deliberately
# absent: they are structural, they follow from whether this environment lists
# docker-compose.cashback.yml in its COMPOSE_FILE, and that overlay sets them
# from container names it chose. Setting them here too would create a second,
# quieter answer to "does this environment run cashback" — and the two would
# disagree the first time one of them was edited in a hurry.
#
# An environment WITHOUT the cashback overlay leaves every key below empty and
# mounts no cashback routes at all. That is the documented safe state.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which affiliate networks this deployment runs, comma-separated, in the order
# they are wired. `fixture` serves a scripted click -> pending -> approved ->
# reversed lifecycle from recorded responses and needs no credential, which is
# what lets the whole money path be built and demonstrated before a publisher
# account is approved (ADR-0003).
#
# Each entry gets a block of settings named after it - NETWORK_FIXTURE_*,
# NETWORK_LINKWISE_*, NETWORK_TRADETRACKER_* - so this file can be read
# straight down and every setting says which network it belongs to.
#
# THE LIST DECIDES, not the presence of a block. A block whose driver is not
# named here is read by nothing, deliberately: the alternative is a wildcard
# lookup that conjures a network from a typo, and a network conjured that way
# has no name to report when its keys are missing.
#
# The flat NETWORK_DRIVER / NETWORK_ACCOUNT_ID / NETWORK_API_KEY /
# NETWORK_API_SECRET / NETWORK_SOURCE_LANGUAGE keys this replaces are REFUSED
# and not aliased. An env file still carrying them fails to start naming them,
# because aliasing lets a deployment that meant to run two networks run one
# with every diagnostic green.
#
# Naming a live network here WITHOUT its account id and credential does not
# stop the api - it stops CASHBACK. See the block below.
NETWORKS=fixture

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The header this host's edge sets to carry the real client address, for the
# privacy-minimised context digest a click records (FR-022) and for the
# per-device half of the click rule (US7 scenario 1).
#
# Empty, and it stays empty until the reverse proxy in front of this api is
# configured to set it AND to strip any inbound copy of it. Until then the
# digest is the proxy's own address: still a context, and not one that tells
# devices apart, so the per-device half of the rule stays off rather than
# bracketing every member behind it. The per-member half is unaffected and
# always applies.
#
# A header a client can set is a context a client can choose, and a chosen
# context evades a per-device rule by changing on every request. Naming one
# the edge does not own would be worse than naming none.
CLICK_CONTEXT_HEADER=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One network's settings. ONE BLOCK PER ENTRY in NETWORKS above, each named
# after its driver: copy this block and swap FIXTURE for LINKWISE,
# TRADETRACKER or AWIN.
#
# These are the keys the binary actually reads
# (internal/platform/config/networks.go). An earlier revision of this file
# invented NETWORK_AWIN_PUBLISHER_ID and NETWORK_AWIN_API_TOKEN and warned
# that nothing read them. The SHAPE is now real - NETWORK_<DRIVER>_* is how
# every network is configured - but those two exact names are still not: the
# suffixes are ACCOUNT_ID, API_KEY, API_SECRET and SOURCE_LANGUAGE, and
# nothing reads PUBLISHER_ID or API_TOKEN under any driver. A deployment key
# that reaches no code is worse than a missing one, because it looks
# configured.
#
# They never enter the database or the repository, and the config package
# wraps each in a Secret so a log line prints whether it is set and never what
# it is.
#
# With NETWORKS naming only `fixture` nothing reads the two credentials and
# empty is correct. Name a live network and its _ACCOUNT_ID and _API_KEY are
# REQUIRED: without them CASHBACK DOES NOT MOUNT. Every cashback route answers
# 404, the startup log names the network and the missing key, and no member
# can click out - cashback moves members' money, and a network that cannot
# poll is one whose transactions never arrive.
#
# The api itself still starts and this host keeps serving the news site. That
# is deliberate and it changed in T215: these keys used to fail config
# parsing, which stopped the whole process, so a typo in one network's key
# would have taken apivo.news down with it - a risk that grows with every
# network added. _API_SECRET is for networks that issue a pair and is never
# required.
#
# NETWORK_<DRIVER>_ACCOUNT_ID is required for the fixture adapter as much as
# for a live one: it names a row of cashback.network_account, which owns the
# two durable cursors. An adapter that needs no credential still polls on
# behalf of somebody.
#
# The row must also be `active` and carry a `backfill_from` - where an account
# nobody has polled starts reading, which nothing invents. Both are operator
# actions against the database, both are one UPDATE, and neither needs a
# restart; until they are done every sweep refuses by name.
#
# Ingestion also needs DATABASE_URL to carry ?pool_max_conns=14. The scheduler
# takes one connection per running job and one for its work, plus two in
# reserve, so the six jobs a fully configured cashback deployment registers -
# the zero-sum check, the payout settlement sweep, the earnings lifecycle,
# the forward and trailing network sweeps and the catalogue import - need
# fourteen where pgx defaults to four. Polling without the catalogue import
# registers five and needs twelve; cashback without either registers three
# and needs eight. The api refuses to start rather than deadlocking under
# load.
#
# QA and Staging must NOT hold production's publisher credentials. A poll is a
# real call against a real publisher account, counted against a real rate
# limit, and a QA environment that reconciles on every merge to `main` would
# spend that budget continuously.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NETWORK_FIXTURE_ACCOUNT_ID=fixture-publisher
NETWORK_FIXTURE_API_KEY=
NETWORK_FIXTURE_API_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NETWORK_<DRIVER>_SOURCE_LANGUAGE - the language THAT network supplies its
# catalogue copy in, as a BCP-47 primary subtag ("de", "en", "el"), as YOU
# state it. Per network, because two networks may supply their copy in two
# languages and one tag for both would put a wrong one on half the catalogue.
#
# Configuration and not detection: no network says what language a programme
# name is in, and a merchant with no copy in the reader's language is shown
# the source language LABELLED as a fallback rather than silently. The label
# has to come from somebody who knows, and a guess puts a wrong language tag
# on every retailer this deployment imports.
#
# With this and BRAND_DIR set, the api imports the whole catalogue every six
# hours in one transaction. With either unset it serves normally and says at
# ERROR that no import is scheduled - and the retailers members can click are
# never refreshed, which looks exactly like a working catalogue until one of
# them has left the network.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NETWORK_FIXTURE_SOURCE_LANGUAGE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOLD_* - the hard rules that hold a suspicious credit for review instead of
# crediting it (US7). Every rule is OFF until its keys are set, and a rule
# that takes a count and a window is refused with only one of them. A held
# credit counts toward nothing the member can see or withdraw and names its
# rule in the review queue until an operator releases or rejects it, with
# their name and a reason. Windows are Go durations ("24h", "720h").
#
#   HOLD_SHARED_CONTEXT_ACCOUNTS + _WINDOW  one device behind this many
#                                            accounts (own included; >= 2);
#                                            needs CLICK_CONTEXT_HEADER
#   HOLD_NEW_ACCOUNT_AGE                     an account younger than this
#   HOLD_SALE_CAP_MINOR + _CURRENCY          a sale at or above this amount
#   HOLD_MEMBER_VELOCITY + _WINDOW           this many credits in the window
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
HOLD_SHARED_CONTEXT_ACCOUNTS=
HOLD_SHARED_CONTEXT_WINDOW=
HOLD_NEW_ACCOUNT_AGE=
HOLD_SALE_CAP_MINOR=
HOLD_SALE_CAP_CURRENCY=
HOLD_MEMBER_VELOCITY=
HOLD_MEMBER_VELOCITY_WINDOW=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BLNK_SECRET_KEY - what the api presents to the ledger on every call.
#
# NOT OPTIONAL on any environment here. Every Apivo environment runs
# APP_ENV=prod, and with cashback enabled the api REFUSES TO START when this
# is unset (internal/platform/config: requireCashbackComplete): "a ledger
# reachable without a credential is a ledger anybody on that network can post
# to, and postings are money".
#
# It must equal BLNK_SERVER_SECRET_KEY in blnk.env. They are two halves of one
# setting - the ledger accepts that value, this one presents it.
#
# What actually makes the ledger CHECK is a third thing: BLNK_SERVER_SECURE,
# set in the compose overlay. Blnk skips authentication entirely when it is
# false (api/middleware/auth.go), so a secret key on its own leaves the ledger
# open to anything that can reach it. All three belong together or the money
# path is unauthenticated while looking configured.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BLNK_SECRET_KEY=
